course: dictionary
lesson: 233
title: Alligator clues
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An alligator is a large reptile with a long body, short legs and a long, wide snout full of strong jaws and many teeth. It lives in rivers, lakes and swamps in the south-east of the USA and in China. When you read about one, the words around it give you clues to what it looks like and where it lives.
explain: Context clues can also tell you how to tell two similar animals apart. «To tell them apart, look at the snout and the teeth.» The alligator has a wide U-shaped snout, while a crocodile has a narrower V-shaped one. The text is comparing them, so you can use each description to work out which animal is which.
explain: Read the story of the boy in Florida: «a bigger one lunged out of the water, and he only just jumped back in time.» He jumped back because the alligator was dangerous, so lunged must mean moved forward suddenly, very fast. Always test your meaning: does «the alligator moved forward suddenly out of the water» still make sense? It does.
fact: An alligator is a reptile: it is cold-blooded, has scaly skin and lays eggs.
fact: «Alligators» live in rivers, lakes and swamps in the south-east USA and in China.
fact: «Gator holes» are places alligators dig in the dry season; they keep water when everything else dries out, and many other animals drink there.
fact: A crocodile has a narrower V-shaped snout than an alligator, and its lower tooth still sticks out when its mouth is shut.
fact: Safety rule: stay well away from the edges of ponds and lakes where alligators may live, and never feed them.
exercise: «To tell them apart, look at the snout and the teeth.» Which snout shape belongs to an alligator? | Round like a ball | Narrow V-shaped | *Wide U-shaped | Pointed like a needle
exercise: «A boy in Florida went close to a pond's edge to look at a small alligator; a bigger one lunged out of the water.» What does lunged most likely mean? | Slept quietly | Swam slowly away | Ate a fish | *Moved forward suddenly
exercise: Which sentence describes an alligator correctly? | A small furry mammal that climbs trees | A bird with feathers and wings | A fish with gills and fins | *A large reptile with a long body and short legs
test: The gator holes «keep water when everything else dries out» in the dry season. What does this tell you about gator holes? | They are only used by crocodiles | They are found only in the sea | They dry up quickly in the sun | *They help many animals survive when water is scarce
test: «Crocodiles and alligators are reptiles with four short legs, a long tail and a body that has hardly changed since their ancestors lived alongside the dinosaurs.» What is the main idea? | Crocodiles have no tails | *Alligators and crocodiles are reptiles with a body shape that has stayed much the same for a very long time | Alligators and crocodiles are fish that swim fast | Dinosaurs were alligators
exam: Which of these would be the safest thing to do near an alligator? | Walk right up to the edge of the pond to take a photo | Feed it a fish from the bank | *Stay well away from the water's edge | Swim across the lake to say hello
